<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fujian</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fukien</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fujian is a mountainous province on the south-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>. Once the land of the Min Yue people, it was brought under Chinese rule by the Han and settled by Chinese from the north over the following centuries. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> its ports, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, became great centres of maritime trade, and many of its people emigrated to Taiwan and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fujian.jpg" alt="Fujian" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lennartbj · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Wuyishan_River_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fujian is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> to the east. The province is mostly mountainous, traditionally described as eight parts mountain, one part water and one part farmland, and its highest point is Mount Huanggang, at 2,157 metres. The name Fujian, 'happy establishment', combines the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and Jianzhou, which is now Nanping. Because of the mountains and successive waves of migration, it is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s most culturally and linguistically diverse provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Keqiutou on Pingtan Island show that Fujian had entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> by the middle of the sixth millennium BCE. The kingdom of Minyue was located there; its name joins Min, perhaps an ethnic name and also that of the main river, with Yue, after the State of Yue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, whose royal family fled to Fujian after Chu annexed that kingdom in 306 BCE. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-wuzhu">Wuzhu</a> built a fortified city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, and his kingdom reached into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and southern Zhejiang.</p><p>A first wave of settlers from the north arrived in the early fourth century, mainly from eight families of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, among them Chen, Lin, Huang and Zheng, which remain the commonest surnames in the province. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a> a second wave came with the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-chao">Wang Chao</a>, Wang Shengui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shenzhi">Wang Shenzhi</a>, who set up a Kingdom of Min with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>. The name Fujian was first used for an administrative region in 985.</p><p>From 1357 to 1366 Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> took part in the Ispah Rebellion before the Yuan crushed it, and the city then lost its foreign trade. Under the Ming, Fujian became a province, and Changle served as the base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s expeditions. A sea trade ban hampered it, and the Qing imposed another ban of about twenty years against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a>'s Ming loyalists on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fujian has a population of about 41.5 million, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> as its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> is its largest prefecture-level city by population, and Xiamen was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first special economic zones. Southern Min, or Hokkien, is spoken by much of the ethnic Chinese population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, because of emigration. The province has four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, among them the Fujian Tulou, earth buildings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>, listed in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fujian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the ports of <b>Fujian</b> it brought Chinese silk, porcelain and copper coins; from Japan swords, lacquerware, fans and copper; and from Southeast Asia sappanwood, pepper and other spices, ivory and aromatic woods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Investors spread their money across many ships, and sea trade spurred an enormous growth in the shipbuilding of <b>Fujian</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It survived in southern China, especially in <b>Fujian</b>, as a secret or semi-Buddhist religion, known as the Religion of Light, into the Ming period; the shrine at Cao’an near Quanzhou, with its image of Mani carved in 1339, remains.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Artefacts closely related to the culture, or believed to belong to it, have been found in Malaysia, Laos and the Chinese provinces of Yunnan, Guangxi and <b>Fujian</b>, and its influence reached maritime Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/dong-son-culture">Dong Son culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2025 autosomal DNA study found the ancient Liangzhu samples intermediate between today’s Han Chinese of Jiangsu and Zhejiang, Kra–Dai speakers and Austronesian speakers of Taiwan and the Philippines, and closer to Neolithic samples from <b>Fujian</b> than to modern Austronesian speakers.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Sino-French War a French squadron destroyed the <b>Fujian</b> fleet and bombarded the navy yard at Fuzhou in August 1884, although Qing armies fought well on land.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
